note {'text': '**By Claude Code** — **Triage (2026-11-24):** A JusPrin 2.0.1 user on Windows pasted an OpenRouter key in Preferences. The key check\'s test request got HTTP 404 from OpenRouter: "No endpoints found that can be served by your own provider keys. This workspace is restricted to BYOK capacity." The key itself is valid; the person\'s OpenRouter workspace is set to route only through their own provider keys (BYOK), and none of their keys covers the model JusPrin asked for.\n**What the user saw:** `ProviderAgent::provider_answer` (src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Agent/ProviderAgent.cpp) knows 401/403, 402/out of credits, too long and 429, but not this 404, so it threw `UnexpectedServiceAnswer`. `AgentHost::pump_setup` reported it here and showed "Something went wrong on our side". The key was not saved, and nothing told the person to change their OpenRouter settings.\n**Why now:** Our code: unchanged; this path has existed since JusPrin 2.0.0 (2026-11-12). Dependencies: none. Client/environment: one person\'s OpenRouter account setting; first time it met the key check. Really new: yes, a new cause, not a regression.\n**Impact:** 1 event, 1 user.\n**Classification:** needs attention. Fix (proposal, not made): recognise OpenRouter\'s 404 "No endpoints found" answers in `provider_answer` and tell the person their OpenRouter account settings (BYOK-only routing, provider or data-policy restrictions) leave no provider for the model, with a link to openrouter.ai settings.'}
